Bootstrap: docker
From: debian:12-slim

# PyMOL, open-source build, for scripted rendering in batch.
#
# Debian's `pymol` package IS the open-source build (2.5.0 in bookworm), which
# is what we want: the Schrodinger incentive build is separately licensed and
# must never be placed on a shared share. Taking it from the distro also means
# the version is pinned by the base tag and the dependency graph is somebody
# else's problem, which for a rendering utility is the right trade.
#
# Build:  chpc-tool build pymol

%labels
    Tool         pymol
    Version      opensource-bookworm
    Upstream     https://github.com/schrodinger/pymol-open-source
    UpstreamRef  debian bookworm pymol package
    Licence      BSD-like (open-source PyMOL; NOT the Schrodinger build)
    Base         debian:12-slim
    Maintainer   Craik Lab, UCSF

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends pymol python3-pymol
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # Assert this is the open-source build and that it runs with no display,
    # which is the only mode it will ever be used in here.
    pymol -cq -d "print('pymol', cmd.get_version()[0])"

%environment
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
    export PYMOL_QUIET=1

%test
    set -eu
    pymol -cq -d "print(cmd.get_version()[0])"

%runscript
    exec pymol "$@"

%help
    Open-source PyMOL, headless.

      chpc-tool exec pymol -- pymol -cq model.pdb -d "hide everything; show cartoon; png out.png, dpi=300, ray=1"

    Always pass -cq (command line, quiet): there is no display on a compute
    node, and without it PyMOL waits for a GUI that never arrives.
